# Generated into a ConfigMap by kustomize (see kustomization.yaml), NOT applied
# as a plain manifest. Nothing in this cluster restarts a pod when a ConfigMap
# it reads changes -- no reloader, no checksum annotation -- so a plain
# ConfigMap consumed with envFrom would change the object and leave the running
# container on the old value. Every switch below is an authentication control,
# where a silent no-op is the worst failure mode. configMapGenerator appends a
# content hash to the name, so Flux rolls the pod on any edit here.

# DON'T CHANGE: the image expects its database and assets under /data, and the
# PVC is mounted there.
DATA_DIR=/data
NEXTAUTH_URL=https://links.pxldi.de
MEILI_ADDR=http://karakeep-meilisearch:7700
# The headless browser is a second container in the same pod, so this is
# localhost. 9223 rather than 9222 because that is the port the image's own
# entrypoint listens on; see the comment on the chrome container.
BROWSER_WEB_URL=http://127.0.0.1:9223
# Renovate proposes image bumps; an in-app update banner would be a second
# source of truth for the same question.
DISABLE_NEW_RELEASE_CHECK=true

# --- Authentication ---
#
# This host is not behind the forward-auth middleware (see ingress.yaml).
# Karakeep speaks OIDC itself, so authentik is the identity provider rather
# than a proxy in front of it.
#
# What these buy is that no endpoint on this host accepts a password:
# authentication happens at auth.pxldi.de instead. That is one login surface
# rather than two, not a hardened one. authentik still has no MFA stage bound
# to default-authentication-flow and no reputation policy, so neither side
# throttles a guess today.
DISABLE_PASSWORD_AUTH=true
OAUTH_PROVIDER_NAME=authentik
# With the password form gone the login page is one button, so skip it.
OAUTH_AUTO_REDIRECT=true
# The slug is the authentik_application slug in terraform/authentik/
# oidc-apps.tf. Karakeep fetches this document and derives every endpoint from
# it, so the URL is per-application, not the global issuer.
OAUTH_WELLKNOWN_URL=https://auth.pxldi.de/application/o/karakeep/.well-known/openid-configuration
OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=lwMBkkGObTNW7kYNLOOFYFL9ji7ReujXiCp4bjnQ

# Deliberately false, and it has to be. apps/web/server/auth.ts refuses the
# sign-in when there is no matching row in the users table and signups are
# disabled:
#
#   if (!user && serverConfig.auth.disableSignups) throw ...
#
# That is every first OAuth login, so turning this on with the password form
# off locks the instance permanently. It is the same trap the app this replaces
# had under a different name (DISABLE_NEW_SSO_USERS).
#
# Signup is not open as a result: the only route in is the authentik
# application, and only members of the karakeep-users group get through it.
# That binding is the membership check.
DISABLE_SIGNUPS=false

# Left off. It links an existing local account to an OAuth identity by email
# alone, which trusts the provider's email claim completely. There are no local
# accounts here to link, since the password form is off from the first boot, so
# it would only ever be a way in if authentik issued a token carrying someone
# else's address.
OAUTH_ALLOW_DANGEROUS_EMAIL_ACCOUNT_LINKING=false

# --- AI ---
#
# Local inference against the cluster's own ollama, so nothing about what gets
# read here leaves the house and there is no per-token bill. The node has no
# GPU, which is what every number below is shaped by. Read core-seconds as "the
# whole machine, briefly" -- 150 of them is about twelve seconds of the box at
# full tilt, once, in the background.
#
# Seven models have now been measured against this instance's real prompt, with
# both custom rules from Settings -> AI included. "bad" counts tags that appear
# nowhere in the article once punctuation is normalised:
#
#   model            tags  bad  tag core-s  summary core-s  summary chars
#   qwen2.5:3b          9    2         162             448           4264
#   granite4.2:3b      11    5         194            1526           7803
#   gemma3:4b          24    5         219             442           2858
#   qwen2.5:7b         15    3         380             627           2133
#   ministral-3:3b     58   45         360             815           4064
#   granite4.2:8b       0    -         518            2409           6966
#   ministral-3:8b     36   20         615            1353           4210
#
# The custom rule says "Return at most 5 tags. Never return more than 5." Every
# model broke it. Newer and bigger both made it worse: granite4.2:8b returned an
# empty array after 518 core-seconds, and ministral-3:3b invented 45 of its 58
# tags. Also unusable: qwen3.5:4b and gemma4:e2b spend the whole output budget
# reasoning and return nothing, and karakeep never passes ollama's think:false.
#
# So qwen2.5:3b, on the only grounds that separate them: fewest tags, fewest
# invented, and the cheapest of the seven. Nine noisy-but-real tags is a usable
# search index; twenty-four with five fabrications is not.
OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://ollama.ollama.svc.cluster.local:11434
INFERENCE_TEXT_MODEL=qwen2.5:3b

# Deliberately unset: INFERENCE_IMAGE_MODEL. qwen2.5:3b cannot read an image at
# all, so turning this on now means keeping a second model resident for it.
# gemma3:4b can, and costs about the same as a link, but the tags it produced
# for a screenshot were plausible and wrong. Nothing runs until an image or PDF
# is saved, so leaving it out costs nothing today.

# Auto-summarization is off, and this is the expensive half of the bill.
#
# No model in the table above wrote the 3-4 plain sentences the prompt asks for.
# All seven produced a markdown essay opening with meta-commentary, against a
# custom rule that forbids exactly that by name -- "Okay, here's a summary of
# the key points", "Based on the information provided", and from granite the
# model narrating its own instructions into the stored field: "Okay, the user
# has shared a lengthy document". Nothing post-processes this. The summary job
# runs with schema: null and the raw response is written to the bookmark row
# verbatim.
#
# It was also the larger cost: 442 to 2409 core-seconds per bookmark against
# 162 for tagging. Turning it off roughly halves what a saved link costs this
# node and removes a field that was worse than empty.
#
# Tags survive because they are graded by a JSON schema and are useful even when
# noisy. Both switches also exist per user under Settings -> AI, along with the
# tag style and the language.
INFERENCE_ENABLE_AUTO_TAGGING=true
INFERENCE_ENABLE_AUTO_SUMMARIZATION=false

# How much of a page the model actually reads. This value is passed to ollama as
# num_ctx, and karakeep hard-truncates the content to fit it (truncateContent in
# apps/workers). The default of 2048 is small enough to be a correctness bug
# here: the first article saved to this instance is 23,215 characters, and every
# tag and every sentence of its summary came from the first 7,400. Two thirds of
# the page never reached the model, and nothing in the UI says so.
#
# 4096 rather than 8192 because prompt processing runs at ~60 tok/s for a 4B
# model on this CPU-only node. 4096 costs roughly 70s of reading per job; 8192
# would put a single job past three minutes for pages that are mostly
# boilerplate anyway.
#
# Do not raise it further. Going 2048 -> 4096 did buy reading depth, from 32% to
# 59% of that article, but it also took gemma from 21 tags to 28: more text in
# the window means more things to tag, and no model here is filtering.
INFERENCE_CONTEXT_LENGTH=4096

# The default of 30s is shorter than a single job takes here. A tagging run is
# ~27s on qwen2.5:3b, plus up to 9s to load the model if it has fallen out of
# memory, so 30 would time out most of the time. Kept at 300 rather than lowered
# with summarization: a slow page on a cold model still needs the headroom.
INFERENCE_JOB_TIMEOUT_SEC=300
# One at a time on purpose. Two concurrent runs would take the whole node.
INFERENCE_NUM_WORKERS=1

# Search vectors, so search matches meaning rather than words.
#
# embeddinggemma was the best of four measured on this node: perfect retrieval
# over six articles including near neighbours (MySQL beside PostgreSQL, Iceland
# beside the Faroes), and it answers German queries against English documents,
# which matters here. 8 core-seconds per bookmark, next to 22 for bge-m3 and 36
# for qwen3-embedding, which also scored worst.
EMBEDDING_TEXT_MODEL=embeddinggemma
# Must match the model. embeddinggemma emits 768; the default of 1536 is
# OpenAI's, and a mismatch silently fills the vector store with wrong-shaped
# rows.
EMBEDDING_DIMENSIONS=768
# Not optional: with OLLAMA_BASE_URL set, karakeep's default for this is false
# (packages/shared/config.ts), so indexing would never run.
EMBEDDING_ENABLE_AUTO_INDEXING=true
EMBEDDING_JOB_TIMEOUT_SEC=120
